3 types of similarities:
1. cosine similarity: the angle (direction) of vectors ignoring the magnitude, the smaller, the more similar
    used to search short queries in long ducoments, the length (magnitude) of them doesnt matter
2. Euclidean distance: the shortest distance between two vectors, magnitude matters
    how to calculate Euclidean distance: say, we have two 2D vectors A = [x1, y1] and B = [x2, y2]
    Euclidean distance = sqrt((x1-x2)^2 + (y1-y2)^2)
    similarity = 1 / (1 + distance)
3. dot product similarity: direction and magnitude both matter, the bigger value, the more similar
    how to calculate Euclidean distance: say, we have two 3D vectors A = [x1, y1, z1] and B = [x2, y2, z2]
    dot product = x1 * x2 + y1 * y2 + z1 * z2
    why it can present similarity: say Query = [1, 2], A = [2, 4], B = [2, -1]
    then Query vs A = 1×2+2×4=10, Query vs B = 1×2+2×(−1)=0, Query · A is higher, so A is more similar to Query
    dot product的几何意义：A的长度*B的长度*AB夹角的cosine值
